On these clean synthetic terrains, adaptive == fixed EXACTLY (same expansions,
cost, mean_msl) at matching angles in all 9 comparisons, because the adaptive
back-off search's steepest candidate (n_min) is already always terrain-safe
here -- there is no obstacle forcing a shallower fallback. Branching (avg
valid successors) does NOT increase monotonically with angle -- it fell at
F's 45 deg (16.00, lower than 10/30's 20.00) because steeper climb/descent
hits the max/min MSL search bound in fewer hops, cutting off some directions
sooner. Expansion count differences across angles are therefore NOT
explained by branching factor; the far more likely driver is state-space
depth/size (shorter horizontal-per-z-step primitives create more distinct
reachable states in the same physical region).

=== SECTION 6: adaptive back-off fallback test (empirical, spike terrain) ===
TEST 1 (steep attempts blocked, generator backs off to a shallower valid one):
  spike=190: attempts=[(1,33.69,False),(2,18.43,False),(3,12.53,True),...] -> chosen=(3, 12.53 deg)
  spike=193: attempts=[(1,33.69,False),(2,18.43,False),(3,12.53,False),(4,9.46,True),...] -> chosen=(4, 9.46 deg)
  spike=195: attempts=[...(1..5 False)...,(6,6.34,True),...] -> chosen=(6, 6.34 deg)
  spike=197/198: all 8 tried n_cells invalid -> chosen=None (correctly declines, no crash, no unsafe pick)
  spike=100 (no real obstruction): n=1 already valid -> chosen=(1, 33.69 deg) immediately (matches "45 valid -> 45 candidate" case)

TEST 2 (max-feasible descent available but a trap follows -- planner not
forced into greedy descent):
  terrain: row 3 is a low band (100) reachable by descent from row 2 (200),
  but row 3 has a wall (elev 400) blocking the low band shortly after entry.
  optimal search result: cost=1.125000, path stayed entirely on row 2
  (LEVEL), never dropped into the trap. Confirms the adaptive generator
  offers max-feasible descent as a CANDIDATE (available to the search) but
  the search is free to -- and did -- prefer LEVEL when descent leads
  somewhere worse overall. No greedy-forced descent behavior observed.

All safety replays across every run in this stage (fixed sweep: 24 optimal
+ 36 ARA* rows; adaptive comparison: 15 configs x 3 phases; F-WIDE: 1
optimal + 3 ARA* phases) came back safe=True, max_angle within the
configured limit. Zero exceptions.

